					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/tunedtoadeadchannel" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(304457); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=304457&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609041009" alt="tunedtoadeadchannel" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=304457)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/tunedtoadeadchannel" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(304457); return false;" >tunedtoadeadchannel</a></span> <span class="odate time_1278992333 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">13 Jul 2010 03:38</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-827767">
									

<p>The biggest draw this site has, and the main thing that sets us apart from simple creepypasta and fanfic, is that the SCP articles and related materials set a believable and realistic tone.</p>
<p>An SCP article is intended to read as a summary of containment procedures and the potential risks of failing to contain an SCP object written by a professional, clinical individual who knows what he's doing.</p>
<p>Lines like "we must have constant vigilance to be sure that the containment center does not become an icy waste." read like fanfic written by a high school student, not like a professional brief written by a serious scientist. They seriously breach character, and strongly damage suspention of disbelief. Frankly, it's a shitty thing to do to your article.</p>
<p>The entire final addendum adds precisely <em>nothing</em> to this article, and takes away quite a bit. You would be better off removing it.</p>
<p>"But someone else fucked up too!" is <em>never</em> a valid excuse.</p>

